{
  "section": "gpu-gemm-tensor-low-precision",
  "addedResources": [],
  "units": [
    {
      "id": "gemm-arithmetic-naive",
      "title": "GEMM operation counts and the naive kernel's traffic",
      "hours": 3,
      "target": [
        "derive"
      ],
      "topics": [
        "gemm-shapes-operations",
        "naive-gemm-mapping"
      ],
      "outcomes": [
        "Derive useful GEMM operations and compulsory bytes for rectangular and batched shapes.",
        "Trace a thread-per-output kernel and distinguish requested loads from DRAM traffic.",
        "Predict how row-major thread mapping changes coalescing without changing the result."
      ],
      "why": "A GEMM speed claim needs an operation count and a traffic model before it needs a timer. The naive kernel makes the cost of missing reuse visible.",
      "next": [
        {
          "unit": "gpu/gpu-gemm-tensor-low-precision/smem-tiled-gemm",
          "note": "Reuse inputs across an output tile."
        }
      ],
      "study": [
        {
          "resource": "aisys-gemm-gpu5",
          "title": "Arithmetic intensity before tiling",
          "url": "https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/ai-systems-performance/9798341627772/",
          "locator": "Chapter 9, opening discussion of arithmetic intensity and Figure 9-1",
          "depth": "Stop before Multilevel Microtiling and Software Prefetching. Treat the roofline as a ceiling, not proof that a kernel reaches either roof."
        },
        {
          "resource": "boehm-sgemm-gpu5",
          "title": "Naive mapping and coalescing",
          "url": "https://siboehm.com/articles/22/CUDA-MMM#kernel-1-naive-implementation",
          "locator": "Kernel 1: Naive Implementation, including Lower Bounding the Fastest Possible Runtime; Kernel 2: Global Memory Coalescing",
          "depth": "Recalculate for your shape. The article's GEMM epilogue and RTX A6000 numbers are not the contract or measured speed of your kernel."
        },
        {
          "resource": "aisys-code-gemm-gpu5",
          "title": "A concrete naive CUDA kernel",
          "url": "https://github.com/cfregly/ai-performance-engineering/blob/b57e4c6a9e261c09ac09208705d040c81b03d35e/code/ch09/baseline_micro_tiling_matmul.cu",
          "locator": "`matmul_naive` and `main`, at commit `b57e4c6`",
          "depth": "Read the square-only kernel and event interval. A checksum is not an independent elementwise correctness test."
        }
      ],
      "focus": [
        "Use conventional shapes \\(A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{M\\times K}\\), \\(B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{K\\times N}\\). Report \\(2MNK\\) useful FLOPs for the multiply using the two-FLOP-per-FMA convention; count epilogue work separately.",
        "For an overwrite-only product with element size \\(s\\), compulsory bytes are \\(Q_{\\min}=s(MK+KN+MN)\\). A nonzero \\(\\beta\\) in \\(C\\leftarrow\\alpha AB+\\beta C\\) adds an old-output read.",
        "The naive overwrite kernel requests \\(2MNK\\) scalar input loads and \\(MN\\) stores. Broadcasts, caches and transaction overfetch make that different from measured DRAM bytes."
      ],
      "reproduce": "Closed-book, derive the counts for an overwrite-only FP32 product with \\(M=64\\), \\(N=96\\), \\(K=32\\). Get 393216 useful FLOPs, 45056 compulsory bytes, and 1597440 requested bytes for the uncached scalar-load model. For independent batches with no shared operands, multiply each count by the batch count.",
      "traps": [
        "Reporting FLOPs as FLOP/s, or requested bytes as measured bandwidth traffic.",
        "Assuming a low arithmetic intensity proves a bandwidth bottleneck. Launch overhead, uncoalesced accesses and dependency chains can prevent reaching the bandwidth roof.",
        "Confusing dimension names across interfaces. LeetGPU Challenge 2 calls the reduction dimension `N`, unlike conventional GEMM notation."
      ],
      "selfTest": [
        {
          "q": "Does an FMA count as one or two FLOPs?",
          "a": "Two in the usual GEMM throughput convention: a multiply and an add, despite being one instruction."
        },
        {
          "q": "Why can the naive kernel read much less DRAM than its scalar-load count predicts?",
          "a": "Several threads request the same values, and caches or warp broadcasts can service those requests without separate DRAM reads."
        },
        {
          "q": "For an overwrite-only square FP32 product with side \\(n\\), what is the compulsory-byte intensity?",
          "a": "\\(I_{\\min}=2n^3/(12n^2)=n/6\\) FLOPs/byte. This assumes each input is read once and the output written once, not that a real kernel achieves it."
        }
      ],
      "practice": [],
      "practiceNote": "The section mini-project covers this: the naive `kernellab` GEMM, its shape-to-FLOP calculator, and separate compulsory-byte and requested-load models.",
      "stopWhen": "You derive the rectangular counts without notes and explain one warp's input and output addresses without calling requested bytes DRAM traffic.",
      "fit": {
        "verdict": "kept",
        "note": "The book's roofline introduction, Boehm's two mappings and the pinned naive kernel cover both topics at derive depth; the mini-project supplies implementation checks."
      }
    },
